WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.122
Function APIs should make memory ownership
visible.

2
00:00:03.202 --> 00:00:07.106
The allocation may happen in one function,
the pointer may be stored by another,

3
00:00:07.186 --> 00:00:09.050
and cleanup may occur later in a third.

4
00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:11.344
If the contract is not clear at each
boundary,

5
00:00:11.424 --> 00:00:15.386
leaks and repeated cleanup become
maintenance risks rather than isolated bugs.

6
00:00:15.466 --> 00:00:18.904
A function that allocates memory should
document who must release it.

7
00:00:18.984 --> 00:00:22.713
If the caller owns the returned pointer,
the function name, documentation,

8
00:00:22.793 --> 00:00:25.123
or surrounding pattern should make that
clear.

9
00:00:25.203 --> 00:00:27.884
If the function returns a pointer to
internal storage,

10
00:00:27.964 --> 00:00:31.809
that should also be clear so callers do
not try to release memory they do not own.

11
00:00:31.889 --> 00:00:35.793
A function that takes ownership should say
whether the caller must stop using or

12
00:00:35.873 --> 00:00:37.621
freeing the pointer after the call.

13
00:00:37.701 --> 00:00:39.740
Ownership transfer is a strong contract.

14
00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.607
Once ownership moves, the old owner should
treat the pointer according to the

15
00:00:43.687 --> 00:00:46.601
documented rule, usually as no longer its
responsibility.

16
00:00:46.681 --> 00:00:50.584
A function that borrows memory should
document how long the pointer must remain

17
00:00:50.664 --> 00:00:51.014
valid.

18
00:00:51.094 --> 00:00:53.891
Borrowed data may need to stay alive only
during the call,

19
00:00:53.971 --> 00:00:57.583
until an operation completes, or until a
containing object is destroyed.

20
00:00:57.663 --> 00:00:59.644
If the callee stores a borrowed pointer,

21
00:00:59.724 --> 00:01:01.880
that retention must be part of the
contract.

22
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.164
Naming conventions such as create and
destroy, init and cleanup,

23
00:01:05.244 --> 00:01:08.624
retain and release, or duplicate and free
can clarify expectations.

24
00:01:08.704 --> 00:01:10.219
Naming is not enough by itself,

25
00:01:10.299 --> 00:01:14.494
but consistent names help reviewers spot
mismatched lifecycle calls and understand

26
00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:15.273
who owns what.

27
00:01:15.353 --> 00:01:19.665
Ambiguous ownership contracts create
leaks, repeated cleanup, and maintenance risk.

28
00:01:19.745 --> 00:01:23.415
A developer who cannot tell whether a
function owns an argument will guess.

29
00:01:23.495 --> 00:01:25.651
A future maintainer may guess differently.

30
00:01:25.731 --> 00:01:29.460
Clear contracts are a low-cost way to
prevent those divergent assumptions.
